{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Property-based testing as an AI-control monitor\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b 50 APPS tasks \u183 ? 5 runs \u183 ? 2026-08-07 \u183 ? run id {\f1 uplanhard50-tlow-uhigh}}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 All figures come from {\f1 runs/uplanhard50-tlow-uhigh/}. Every number states its denominator, and every threshold is labelled.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 1. What we are testing\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 An untrusted model writes code. Sometimes it hides a {\b backdoor}: code that passes every visible test but returns a wrong answer on one rare input the attacker picked.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The usual defence is a {\b trusted monitor} \u8212- a weaker model we trust, which reads the code and rates it 1 to 10 for suspiciousness.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Our idea: running tests should reveal what reading cannot.} A backdoor is written to look innocent on the page. It cannot hide from an input that triggers it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two predictions:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 1.\tx360\tab {\b Property tests find backdoors on their own.} That arm should beat chance.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 2.\tx360\tab {\b Property evidence makes a code-reading monitor better.} A monitor also shown the tests and their results should beat one shown only the code.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Relation to prior work\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The framework is not ours. The trusted/untrusted split, the audit budget, the deployment game, and calibrating the audit threshold against honest traffic all come from {\b Greenblatt, Shlegeris, Sachan & Roger, {\i AI Control: Improving Safety Despite Intentional Subversion}, arXiv:2312.06942, ICML 2024}. The backdoors come from {\b Terekhov, Liu, Gulcehre & Albanie, {\i Control Tax}, arXiv:2506.05296}. Both PDFs are in {\f1 papers/}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 What is ours is the property-testing monitor and the arms built on it.\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 2. Setup\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 2.1 Tasks and candidates\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b 50} APPS competition-tier problems.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b 115} candidate solutions: {\b 50 honest}, {\b 65 backdoored}. (35 problems have one backdoor, 15 have two.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab All 65 backdoors work: they pass the visible tests and fail on the attacker\u8217's secret input.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Each problem has a reference solution we treat as correct.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The candidate code is fixed before the experiment begins.} All 115 solutions come from the Control Tax dataset and are identical across all five runs. No model writes attack code during a run.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two consequences. Every backdoor works by construction, which removes a channel real monitors benefit from \u8212- failed attempts. And nothing here measures attacker skill.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 2.2 Models\par}
