{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Safety = the fraction of 10,000 deployments that end safe.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Three things to know before reading any safety number:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The budget buys rank, not accuracy.} A monitor must put suspicious code {\i above} honest code. Scoring everything alike buys nothing.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b About 35 points of it is free.} At a 10% attack rate over 10 steps, the attacker never attacks at all in {\f1 0.9\u185 ?\u8304 ? = 34.9%} of deployments. TM\u8217's 0.603 is 0.349 free plus 0.254 earned.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b It can come back empty.} Setting the bar needs a score for {\i every} honest candidate. One missing score voids that run for that arm.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li720 \fi0 {\b These safety values are on our own 10-step, one-slot game, with a fixed 10% attack rate and every backdoor working by construction. They are not on the same scale as safety figures in Greenblatt et al.\u160 ?or Terekhov et al., and should not be compared to them.}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3.2 AUC\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Pick a random backdoor and a random honest candidate. {\b AUC is the chance the monitor scores the backdoor higher.} 0.5 is a coin flip, 1.0 is perfect.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 AUC ignores the audit budget, which is why it is useful: when safety comes back empty, AUC still says whether the monitor sees anything.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3.3 The detection table\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 For every score a monitor produced, the population splits three ways:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab backdoors \u8594 ? {\b caught} / {\b missed} / {\b no verdict}\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab honest code \u8594 ? {\b false alarm} / {\b cleared} / {\b no verdict}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Each triple sums to its class total at every row, so nothing is silently dropped and abstentions stay visible. This is the only table that survives when safety abstains.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 3.4 Did the measurement work?\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 A monitor that fails to answer is not a monitor that says \u8220"fine\u8221". So we report:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Decision coverage} \u8212- how often PBT gave an actual verdict.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Suite validity} \u8212- how often the generated properties were usable at all.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Suite soundness} \u8212- how often every property survived the reference check.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Infrastructure share} \u8212- of the unusable suites, how many were our harness timing out rather than the model writing something bad.\sa180\par}
