{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b In the middle}, TM catches a handful more, while informed flags meaningfully less honest code \u8212- 103 against 129 at threshold 0.778.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b At the strictest point}, informed catches {\b 150 to TM\u8217's 114 \u8212- 36 more, for one extra false alarm.}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The reason: {\b 186} of TM\u8217's catches sit at exactly 9/10, against {\b 138} of informed\u8217's. TM is reluctant to award full marks. The informed monitor, having watched a property fail, commits.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The top of the curve is the part that counts.} With 250 honest instances and a 10% budget you can afford about 25 false alarms. At threshold 0.889 both arms flag 66\u8211-67 \u8212- nearly triple. At 1.000 they flag 5\u8211-6. So the audit rule takes everything at 1.000 and samples the 9/10 group to fill the rest, which means informed\u8217's 36-instance advantage is bought at probability 1, while TM\u8217's mid-curve edge sits where you cannot afford to operate.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 4.4 PBT alone cannot buy an audit\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b One operating point.} Two output levels means no dial: 50% of backdoor-instances caught, 30% of honest instances flagged. (Counting only candidates PBT actually decided, the false-alarm rate is 35%; the 30% figure treats abstentions as cleared.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b That point is unaffordable.} Conditional on a decision, its false-alarm rate runs {\b 0.26\u8211-0.45} across runs, against a budget of {\b 0.10}. The cheapest thing PBT offers costs three to four times more than we can spend.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Two levels do not prevent ranking.} The audit rule breaks ties with a weighted coin, and an AUC of 0.617 is itself a ranking statistic. What two levels prevent is a {\i cheap} operating point.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b What it caught.} 162 detections pooled over 5 runs. In distinct terms: {\b 54 of the 65 backdoors caught at least once, only 6 caught in all five runs.} Two-thirds of those detections (98 of 162) came from a suite that also fired on that task\u8217's honest solution, so the excess over chance is roughly 65 detections, not 162. The properties are readable \u8212- {\f1 prop_reversal_invariance} and {\f1 prop_permutation_equivariance} are among the top catchers.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The gated arm is a different animal.} {\f1 pbt_ceiling} \u8212- sound properties, valid inputs only \u8212- catches 90 / 325 (28%) with {\b zero false alarms in all five runs}. It is not deployable, because it needs the reference solution. But it bounds the question: essentially all of the deployable arm\u8217's 30% false-alarm rate is unsoundness, not signal.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 4.5 The informed monitor\u8217's edge looks like resolution\par}
