{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi0 The same ten problems gave {\b +0.0371} in an earlier run under a nominally identical setup. The sign flips between replications, and the swing between them (0.066) exceeds the gap between the subsets (0.049). Across 400 random 10-task subsets of this run, roughly 37% come out negative.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi0 That subset was labelled a {\i \u8220"hardest-for-TM proxy\u8221"}, but TM separates {\b better} there (0.933 against 0.855), so the label is not supported.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Compute cost is not measured.} The PBT arms generate properties, generate inputs, and execute tests for every candidate. TM reads code. A 0.023 gain for that much compute may be a bad trade, and this evaluation cannot say.\sa180\par}
{\pard \qc \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\emdash\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 5. Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b The clear result is negative: property-based testing is not a drop-in replacement for a code-reading monitor.} It finds real backdoors \u8212- 54 of 65 caught at least once \u8212- but at 30% false alarms, with a single operating point costing three to four times the audit budget. Scored generously it earns 0.07 safety where a code-reading monitor earns 0.24.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The interesting result is the {\i shape} of the positive one. Giving property evidence to a monitor that can rank produces a gain concentrated at the strict end, exactly where the budget operates, and the mechanism looks like better {\b resolution} rather than better accuracy \u8212- 9.6 score levels against 6.8, and five times less bunching at the top. If that holds, the value of execution evidence is not that it knows things reading doesn\u8217't. It\u8217's that it lets the monitor {\b commit}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two consequences:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b PBT needs a graded score} \u8212- how many properties fired, on how many inputs, how confidently. Turning two levels into a distribution is the difference between an unusable arm and a rankable one. More tasks will not fix it; this is a method change.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Abstention limits what we can measure.} One candidate in seven gets no verdict, and all-or-nothing calibration turns that into losing the arm\u8217's headline entirely.\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel2 \b \fs28 Blocking instead of auditing \u8212- already has results\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Auditing spends a scarce resource. {\b Blocking} does not: refuse to ship code a property caught, and fall back to the trusted model\u8217's solution. There is no budget. The cost is paid in what you ship.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 That inverts the economics in PBT\u8217's favour, because a blocking rule doesn\u8217't need to {\i rank}. It only needs to be {\b right when it fires}.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 This is Greenblatt et al.\u8217's {\b defer-to-trusted} protocol (arXiv:2312.06942, Table 2), already implemented here in {\f1 deployment_game.py} and already run on the 40-task held-out split ({\f1 reports/deployment_heldout40.json}):\par}
